{"resourceIdentifier":"5511","resourceName":"Brazil Cerrado 1","description":"The Brazil Cerrado 1 Project is developed pursuant to a reforestation strategy that seeks to generate risk-adjusted returns and GHG removals primarily through the protection and/or restoration of natural forests across ~50% of biologically productive land area, and the establishment of Forest Stewardship Council (FSC) certified sustainably managed commercial tree farms across the remaining ~50%.\r\n\r\nThe Brazil Cerrado 1 Project will produce GHG removals by reforesting degraded pastureland in the Brazilian states of Mato Grosso do Sul, Mato Grosso, and Minas Gerais, largely focused on the Cerrado region. Reforestation will occur through restoration of natural forests (via natural regeneration, assisted natural regeneration and native tree planting) and by planting sustainably managed FSC-certified Eucalyptus tree farms (E. Urophylla x E. grandis hybrids) managed on an extended rotation as compared to business-as-usual practices in the region.\r\n\r\nCattle ranching is the dominant land use in the project’s target region. Cattle ranchers often introduce non-native grasses (e.g. Brachiaria sp.), and any native vegetation that naturally regenerates is grazed by cattle. Once every 3-5 years, ranchers typically use tractors to mechanically remove any woody vegetation that has survived grazing, and the cycle begins again. This leads to a landscape that sequesters minimal carbon and is largely devoid of the natural Cerrado biome flora structure.\r\n\r\nThrough this project, woody vegetation will be re-established by removing grazing cattle, planting Eucalyptus spp., and restoring and permanently protecting native habitat areas. This will increase the native Cerrado cover in the area while also increasing carbon sequestration and wood products production in the area.","location":{"latitude":-18.976718,"longitude":-53.542828},"inPublicCommentPeriod":false,"lastPublicCommentPeriod":{"startInclusiv